foreign  [Music]  this is your Tech news briefing for  Tuesday November 29th I'm Zoe Thomas for  The Wall Street Journal  last year Facebook parent meta began  releasing quarterly reports on widely  viewed content basically summaries of  the top 20 posts that were viewed most  on its platform the goal was to show the  range of content going viral on Facebook  what it ended up showing was that  virtually all of the top ranked content  was spammy over sexualized or generally  with the company classified as  regrettable  Not only was it an embarrassment for the  company Executives according to sources  and documents reviewed by The Wall  Street Journal it also threatened to  throw off Facebook's plans to change  users news feeds  the company got to work and the latest  report released last week shows only one  of the top 20 posts qualified as  engagement bait  so how did Facebook deal with the  problem and what can other social media  platforms learn from its efforts  joining us to discuss this is our  technology reporter Jeff Horowitz so  Jeff what kind of content are we talking  about when Facebook said it had too much  quote regrettable content on people's  feeds so this is a definitional issue in  the regrettable as it was originally  conceived by the company was like  engagement bait things that violated the  rules it was I think a fairly  restrictive definition stuff they didn't  want at the top of the platform so they  were moving to adopt reels which is a  short form video product and instead of  seeing things that were reshared by  people you knew from pages so much they  were going to be directly recommending  entertaining videos it's very much like  tick tock and some of the things I think  that they had initially assumed which is  that the kind of low quality content  things that have bad lighting or like  unclear what's going on people's filming  a video on a TV screen you know things  that just look terrible as well as our  quote unquote gross or a waste of time  those things weren't captured by their  initial definition of regrettable and so  they had to expand it how did all this  content come to you know be at the top  of users feed on Facebook so all of it  really thrived in the ecosystem Facebook  setup right which is that the  distribution of content is governed by  how people engage with it you know if  nobody is liking sharing it commenting  on it it's not going to go anywhere on  the platform and what the company has I  think been coming to conclude in the  last few years and and certainly is  present in this work is that maybe just  because you get a rise out of people and  get a response doesn't mean they  actually want the content in question  and so there was a concern that in  particular with reels that the content  quality was going to be really a problem  one of the things I think that was a big  Focus was quote unquote aggression and  that was videos of people fighting  whether like physically in a Walmart or  screaming at each other on the street or  what have you videos of people being  angry and nasty to each other really did  well and they had to figure out how to  address that and make sure that that  wasn't going to be a lot of what was in  there and Facebook really had to think  about that because it's focused on  making this pivot to Reels its short  form video where it's competing against  Tick Tock right  exactly for a number of years the  company's position on content quality  was look like we don't get to choose  what people like you know whatever Rises  to the top is represents the People's  Choice was kind of the attitude about  this internally and it turns out that  that may have been a little bit  misplaced and particularly when it came  to something that was more like Tick  Tock which is reals I don't think the  company was looking forward to being  responsible for those recommendations if  that makes sense like this wasn't stuff  that someone's friends had liked this  was just sort of Facebook saying hey you  should check out this content and then  it would be a whole bunch of fight  videos  all right so let's talk about Facebook's  reaction to this what is it done to try  and remove this kind of content from  going viral so at the beginning of this  year the company put together a Content  quality war room which was product and  design and integrity which is kind of  the user safety division all working  together to try to Define what makes for  a garbage video and post and to figure  out how to implement that and code that  into Facebook systems so trying to  prevent the worst type of content from  getting to the top basically and how do  we know this  so that all came from internal documents  you know I think the company has sort of  walked a line between wanting to  acknowledge that content quality on  Facebook is a priority and that there  have been in some improvements and also  not wanting to acknowledge how bad the  uh problem was right so Facebook did  release its widely viewed content report  almost all the content on it is just  fine very little engagement bait it's  nothing particularly scandalous or awful  the thing they left out of that at  Public Announcement which they did  acknowledge internally and some of the  documents I've seen was that pretty much  all of it like literally 100 percent of  the content in the top 20 posts from a  year ago was low quality or regrettable  you you know we've been having a lot of  conversations on this show a lot of  people have been talking more widely  about social media content and content  moderation is there anything that other  platforms or even Facebook itself could  learn from this experience  yeah over a number of years Facebook has  had to be think its initial position  that people engage with was what they  wanted that was always the company's  assumption that intervening would be  biased because if something was getting  re-shared broadly that must mean that  people really wanted to see it and  I think time and time again the company  saw examples it was kind of embarrassed  by what was at the top of the chart and  over time I think it started developing  metrics for what users valued and it  turns out that what users reacted to  wasn't the same thing that what users  valued and so I think when we were  looking at say Twitter and Elon Musk  right now the whole idea of I want this  to be a platform for free speech and I  want it to be something that's  newsworthy and reliable and trustworthy  all of those things it doesn't just  happen on its own it seems like what  Facebook's learned is that you have to  go into this stuff with some level of  values not whether it's you know whether  one should or shouldn't be able to say a  particular thing but just like  that you can't use sort of spammy  content and engagement tricks to beat  the algorithm and get to the top you  have to do something to protect yourself  against that all right that's our  reporter Jeff Horowitz Jeff thanks so  much for joining us certainly thank you  for having me and that's it for today's  Tech news briefing if you're looking for  more Tech stories check  site dub dubsj.com  The Wall Street Journal thanks for  listening  [Music]  [Music]

